WEBVTT

00:00:08.918 --> 00:00:13.338
Well, it's been good to meet you Sam, and
very interesting to hear about your business.

00:00:13.338 --> 00:00:17.598
Look, we are having a small dinner for some
of our clients and friends after this. Why

00:00:17.598 --> 00:00:19.048
don't you join us?

00:00:19.048 --> 00:00:23.048
That's very kind of you. I'll just check with
my associate whether they have other arrangements

00:00:23.048 --> 00:00:23.807
for us.

00:00:23.807 --> 00:00:26.548
Your associate is most welcome to join us
too.

00:00:26.548 --> 00:00:27.798
Thank you - excuse me.

00:00:27.798 --> 00:00:34.798
This is Sam Eriks from Eriks imports. He has
very kindly invited us to a dinner.

00:00:37.267 --> 00:00:39.148
Yes, would you like to join us?

00:00:39.148 --> 00:00:42.918
Unfortunately I have another engagement, but
thank you for the invitation.

00:00:42.918 --> 00:00:46.968
Well, perhaps you could join us after that
for a drink?

00:00:46.968 --> 00:00:51.087
Sounds great, I'd be happy to. Where shall
we meet?

00:00:51.087 --> 00:00:54.227
How about the lounge bar here, at about ten?

00:00:54.227 --> 00:00:57.568
I'll see you then. Excuse me.

00:00:57.568 --> 00:00:59.818
Lin. Victor's joining us for dinner.

00:00:59.818 --> 00:01:01.368
Oh wonderful.

00:01:01.368 --> 00:01:02.528
I hope you don't mind.

00:01:02.528 --> 00:01:05.358
Of course not, you're most welcome.

00:01:05.358 --> 00:01:07.338
Well, shall we make a move?

00:01:07.338 --> 00:01:10.237
Would you mind if I just say goodbye to a
few people?

00:01:10.237 --> 00:01:12.788
No problem - we'll see you outside in a few
minutes.

00:01:12.788 --> 00:01:19.788
Okay.

00:01:19.888 --> 00:01:26.888
Let's look at invitations. How do you go about
inviting someone to something? How does Sam

00:01:26.918 --> 00:01:30.948
invite Victor to his dinner?
Look, we're having a small dinner for some

00:01:30.948 --> 00:01:34.797
of our clients and friends after this. Why
don't you join us?

00:01:34.797 --> 00:01:41.657
Spoken invitations in this kind of situation
are semi-formal. It's more like a suggestion

00:01:41.657 --> 00:01:48.467
than a formal invitation. There are a few
different phrases you can use. Practise them

00:01:48.467 --> 00:01:54.398
with Sam.
Why don't you join us?

00:01:54.398 --> 00:01:58.538
Perhaps you could join us?

00:01:58.538 --> 00:02:03.568
It would be great if you could join us.

00:02:03.568 --> 00:02:10.298
Would you like to join us?
Notice that even though, "It would be great

00:02:10.298 --> 00:02:17.298
if you could join us" is not in a question
form, it is still being used as a question.

00:02:17.308 --> 00:02:24.308
Notice also how we use could and would in
polite invitations, not can or will. How does

00:02:26.598 --> 00:02:29.708
Victor reply?
That's very kind of you.

00:02:29.708 --> 00:02:35.898
He says, "That's very kind of you."
When replying to an invitation, first thank

00:02:35.898 --> 00:02:41.958
the other person for the invitation, then
give your answer. Here's Victor with some

00:02:41.958 --> 00:02:47.218
different ways of doing this:
Thank you.

00:02:47.218 --> 00:02:50.818
That's very kind of you.

00:02:50.818 --> 00:02:57.718
That sounds good.
And you can put them all together, like this:

00:02:57.718 --> 00:03:00.498
Thank you.
That's very kind of you.

00:03:00.498 --> 00:03:05.187
Sounds good.
After thanking the person who asked, you need

00:03:05.187 --> 00:03:12.178
to give an answer, either accept the invitation,
or don't accept the invitation, or give a

00:03:12.178 --> 00:03:16.568
reason why you can't answer. What does Victor
do?

00:03:16.568 --> 00:03:20.528
That's very kind of you. I'll just check with
my associate whether they have other arrangements

00:03:20.528 --> 00:03:26.178
for us.
Victor says he has to check with someone else.

00:03:26.178 --> 00:03:32.408
If there are other arrangements, Victor would
make an excuse. When refusing an invitation,

00:03:32.408 --> 00:03:38.608
you should give a reason. Look at this:
Would you like to join us?

00:03:38.608 --> 00:03:39.798
No thanks.

00:03:39.798 --> 00:03:46.158
Oh - right.
It's rude just to say no without a reason,

00:03:46.158 --> 00:03:49.818
and the reason should be a good one. Look
again.

00:03:49.818 --> 00:03:51.538
Would you like to join us?

00:03:51.538 --> 00:03:54.558
No thanks, it doesn't sound very interesting.

00:03:54.558 --> 00:04:00.967
Oh.
Sam would rightly be offended by that reply.

00:04:00.967 --> 00:04:05.717
So what are some ways of making a polite excuse?
Listen to Walter.

00:04:05.717 --> 00:04:07.618
Yes, would you like to join us?

00:04:07.618 --> 00:04:11.357
Unfortunately I have another engagement, but
thank you for the invitation.

00:04:11.357 --> 00:04:16.778
Walter doesn't need to say what his other
engagement, or appointment is - just that

00:04:16.778 --> 00:04:23.778
he has already made another commitment. Notice
that he still thanks Sam for the invitation.

00:04:24.847 --> 00:04:29.198
Practise with Walter some other ways of making
an excuse.

00:04:29.198 --> 00:04:34.497
Unfortunately I have another engagement.

00:04:34.497 --> 00:04:36.927
I'm afraid I have another commitment.

00:04:36.927 --> 00:04:43.927
I can't I'm sorry. Perhaps another time?
By saying, "Perhaps another time", Walter

00:04:46.888 --> 00:04:53.647
is being polite and friendly, rather than
just declining the invitation. Notice that

00:04:53.647 --> 00:04:59.897
he says, "I can't". This implies that he has
another commitment, without having to say

00:04:59.897 --> 00:05:06.007
what that commitment is. This is acceptable
in business.

00:05:06.007 --> 00:05:10.638
Sam and Walter make another arrangement. Watch
how they do this.

00:05:10.638 --> 00:05:12.837
Perhaps you can join us after that for a drink?

00:05:12.837 --> 00:05:18.638
That sounds great. I'd be happy to. Where
shall we meet?

00:05:18.638 --> 00:05:21.777
How about the lounge bar here, about ten?

00:05:21.777 --> 00:05:25.847
I'll see you then.
Sam says, "Perhaps you could join us after

00:05:25.847 --> 00:05:30.497
that for a drink?"
By saying perhaps he is leaving the invitation

00:05:30.497 --> 00:05:37.497
open. Walter is under no pressure to accept.
Practise these phrases using perhaps with

00:05:38.548 --> 00:05:45.548
Sam.
Perhaps you'd like to join us later?

00:05:45.677 --> 00:05:51.627
Perhaps you'd be interested in meeting us
for breakfast?

00:05:51.627 --> 00:05:58.627
Perhaps we could meet later in the week?
Notice that Sam says you'd. Perhaps "you'd

00:06:01.287 --> 00:06:08.048
like to join us later".
You'd is short for you would.

00:06:08.048 --> 00:06:13.407
Walter accepts and they make the arrangement.
Notice the slightly less formal way Walter

00:06:13.407 --> 00:06:17.907
accepts.
That sounds great. I'd be happy to. Where

00:06:17.907 --> 00:06:21.837
shall we meet?
You can use these simple phrases to accept

00:06:21.837 --> 00:06:28.707
an invitation. Practise them.
That sounds great.

00:06:28.707 --> 00:06:30.768
Sounds good.

00:06:30.768 --> 00:06:33.457
I'd love to.

00:06:33.457 --> 00:06:40.457
I'd be happy to.
Walter says, "Where shall we meet?" You can

00:06:40.777 --> 00:06:47.777
use shall or will, but shall we is a common
usage in questions, simply because it is easier

00:06:48.317 --> 00:06:55.317
to say than will we. Shall is also used when
asking for, or making suggestions. Let's look

00:06:56.787 --> 00:07:02.548
at how Sam makes the arrangement:
How about the lounge bar here, at about ten?

00:07:02.548 --> 00:07:07.108
I'll see you then.
By asking, "Where shall we meet?" Walter is

00:07:07.108 --> 00:07:14.108
leaving the details of the arrangement up
to Sam. Sam gives a place and a time, but

00:07:14.168 --> 00:07:20.027
because this is an informal meeting, he doesn't
make it sound like an appointment.

00:07:20.027 --> 00:07:26.057
He says, "How about the lounge bar?"
How about invites the other person to say

00:07:26.057 --> 00:07:32.687
if it is not convenient.
And he says, "At about ten?" as a question.

00:07:32.687 --> 00:07:39.127
This also leaves room for the other person
to suggest a different time. Practise these

00:07:39.127 --> 00:07:45.757
two phrases with Sam. Listen carefully to
Sam's voice, and whether he uses a rising

00:07:45.757 --> 00:07:51.198
or falling tone.
How about the lounge bar here?

00:07:51.198 --> 00:07:55.297
About ten?
Walter confirms the arrangement by saying,

00:07:55.297 --> 00:08:02.007
"I'll see you then." This is now a definite
commitment, and an end to the arrangement.

00:08:02.007 --> 00:08:08.037
Notice how the stress is on the word then,
to confirm that the time is definite. Try

00:08:08.037 --> 00:08:15.037
saying this: "I'll see you then."
Let's have a look now at the end of the scene.

00:08:17.108 --> 00:08:21.807
There are some more useful phrases.
Mr Tang is joining us for dinner.

00:08:21.807 --> 00:08:23.517
Oh wonderful.

00:08:23.517 --> 00:08:24.587
I hope you don't mind.

00:08:24.587 --> 00:08:27.548
Of course not, you're most welcome.

00:08:27.548 --> 00:08:29.527
Well, shall we make a move?

00:08:29.527 --> 00:08:32.308
Would you mind if I just say goodbye to a
few people?

00:08:32.308 --> 00:08:37.368
No problem, we'll see you outside in a few
minutes. Okay.

00:08:37.368 --> 00:08:42.668
Victor says, "I hope you don't mind". It's
a polite way of asking for someone else's

00:08:42.668 --> 00:08:49.668
approval but Lin can't really say no, since
Victor is listening. But Lin politely confirms

00:08:50.368 --> 00:08:57.358
the arrangement. Practise how she does this:
Of course not.

00:08:57.358 --> 00:09:03.077
You're most welcome.
What are the main points we've learned today.

00:09:03.077 --> 00:09:09.067
When inviting someone informally, make it
sound like a suggestion. That way, if someone

00:09:09.067 --> 00:09:15.838
can't accept, they don't feel so bad. "Why
don't you join us?" instead of, "I invite

00:09:15.838 --> 00:09:18.108
you to join us."

00:09:18.108 --> 00:09:23.947
And if you need to make an excuse, don't just
refuse an invitation - give a reason, and

00:09:23.947 --> 00:09:30.947
apologise. "I'm sorry, I have another commitment."
When confirming details, use an upward inflection.

00:09:33.447 --> 00:09:40.298
"About ten." sounds like an order. But, "About
ten?" is asking whether it's convenient for

00:09:40.298 --> 00:09:43.048
the other person.

00:09:43.048 --> 00:09:46.058
That's all for today on the Business of English.
See you next time.